Threat Model
This tool processes employee data that includes names, emails, employee IDs, departments, and salaries. 
The main threats come from the possibility of exposing personal information during processing or from someone trying to reverse the anonymisation methods.

Assets
Original CSV containing personal data
Encryption key
Anonymised output
Logs and privacy reports

Threat Actors
Someone with access to the raw dataset
Someone trying to reverse hashed or tokenised values
An internal user misusing the tool
An attacker gaining access to output files or logs

Potential Threats
Exposure of raw data if the original CSV is stored or shared incorrectly
Key leakage, which would allow encrypted salary values to be decrypted
Hash comparison attacks, where identical hashes reveal identical emails
Token misuse, where someone tries to map tokens back to real employee IDs
Log leakage, if logs accidentally contain sensitive information
Misconfiguration, where fields are anonymised incorrectly or not at all

Vulnerabilities
The encryption key is stored locally and must be protected
Hashing preserves patterns, which can reveal duplicates
Tokenisation relies on a mapping dictionary that must not be exposed
Logs could reveal more than intended if not handled carefully

Controls Implemented
Names are fully redacted
Emails are hashed using SHA‑256
Employee IDs are replaced with non‑identifying tokens
Salaries are encrypted using Fernet
Logs are kept simple and can be ignored via .gitignore
Configuration is separated from code to reduce mistakes
Synthetic data option avoids using real data entirely

Residual Risk
Even after anonymisation, some risk remains. Unique salary values or rare departments could still hint at specific individuals in very small organisations.
Hashing also keeps identical values consistent, which is useful for testing but still reveals patterns. 
As long as the encryption key and token mappings are kept private, the remaining risk is low.

Overall Assessment
The tool reduces the dataset’s risk from medium to low by removing direct identifiers and protecting sensitive fields. 
When used correctly — with the encryption key secured and logs handled safely — the anonymised output is suitable for testing and development without 
exposing personal information.
